== Findings and fixes (each reviewer test reproduced on 7b3b7d2, passes after the fix in on and ft)

1. Walk-fault record taken by an EX capture (535975a). Reviews: hlv_wf_directed test 2, hlv_ctx_directed
   test 7 (HSV, HLVX), mem53_wf_hlv_directed test 1.
   A vle64ff whose element k > 0 walks and page-faults trims vl with no trap; the record {wf_va, page,...}
   stays live while MEM presents that VA. The next instruction, an HLV/HSV/HLVX of the same VA (guest
   translation valid), is in EX while a bubble with another VA is in MEM. g_dreg's dl_* lookup of EX's access
   matched the record by VA only (port_result) and captured the satp-regime page fault into mt_q; main looks
   the access up in MEM a cycle later, after the record dropped (MEM VA != wf_va), and loads/stores.
   7b3b7d2 on/ft: hlv_wf test 2, hlv_ctx test 7, mem53_wf_hlv test 1 FAIL with random bus timing (both seeds;
   no-cache passes: slower fetch, no EX/MEM overlap).
   Fix: mt_valid_q <= dl_vld_i && (dl_mem_i || !dl_wf). A capture of EX's access that matches the record is
   not valid, so MEM replays its own lookup (+1 cycle, only when EX's VA equals a live data walk-fault
   record); MEM's own captures follow main's rule unchanged (the record applies while MEM presents its VA).
   The simulation check (mt_q vs MEM's own lookup) used to exempt every capture that matched a record,
   which hid this; it now exempts only MEM's own captures whose record dropped before use (e.g. at a fetch
   walk start). Mutation: with the fix reverted (mt_valid_q <= dl_vld_i) the check fires on hlv_ctx,
   hlv_wf and mem53_wf_hlv (random bus, both seeds; 60/66 kept runs pass, the other 6 stop on the check),
   and the new mmu_tb check fails (mmu_tb.cpp:215).
   Main keeps its own weakness (the record is not tagged with regime/access class): review B's probe
   with the HLV directly behind the trim (mem53_wf_hlv_dense_probe, not kept) gives the same verdicts on
   main d7fb9a2 (+vro), on the fixed branch off/on/ft and on 7b3b7d2: FAIL test 1 with random bus (seeds 1,
   7), PASS no-cache. For a main issue (tag the record with access class and regime).

2. Stale GPA in mtval2/htval after a fetch access fault (3ad5408). Review: mem53_ifault_gpa_directed.
   g_ireg drove if_fault_guest_o/if_fault_gpa_o from ir_q (the previous fetch lookup) without fault gating;
   sigil_core latches ifid_gpa_q <= mmu_if_fault_guest ? gpa : 0 on every fetch. A PMP fetch access fault
   (cause 1) right after a lookup that was a guest-page fault wrote (2^41+8)>>2 to mtval2 (spec: 0).
   7b3b7d2 on/ft: FAIL test 1 in all three bus settings. Fix: if_fault_guest_o = if_fault_o && ir_q.guest
   (main's MMU raises the guest flag only with the fault).

3. False alarm of the it_q simulation check (97f3dce, simulation only). Reviews: mem53_itlb_remap_directed,
   vm_stale_fetch_directed. A code page remapped to an identical copy without SFENCE, then re-walked for a
   store's D bit, puts the new translation in the TLB while it_q holds the old one for a cycle; either is
   legal until SFENCE. 7b3b7d2 on/ft: "fetch page register differs from the I-port lookup" in all bus
   settings. The check now skips cycles in which it_q was loaded before the last TLB change (ichg), until
   it_q reloads.

Unit TB (08c607a): mmu_tb's FPGA_TIMING build drives dl_mem_i (set while MEM's access is presented, clear
for the capture before it, as the core). Found by the first gate attempt at d8d5e92: make -C sim mmu
failed at mmu_tb.cpp:194 (every capture looked like EX's, so the record never applied). New DREG-only check
(3 checks, MMU TB 77 checks [FPGA_TIMING]): with a data walk fault unconsumed, an EX capture of that VA is
not valid and MEM's replay takes the record.

